Z||000000||hutch84fierose||12-05-2001||07:57 PM||||I bought a nose from fiero conversions in windsor ontario.  I have an 84 se fiero and i heard and was told that an 85 gt nose would fit no problem and the groves match.<BR>I got the nose today took my nose off tonight and went to put it on.  First of all the inner wheel wells don't line up really well there is a space in there so they don't fit.  theres nothing to really mount it to.  I was told this was a take off bolt on deal so i figured no problem this isn't going to be a problem what has to be changed for this to fit properly????  Has anyone ever done this????<BR>Thanks for the help<BR>jon hutcheson||||reg||1
Z||000001||tomanyfieros||12-05-2001||08:11 PM||||u have to get (if im not mistaking) different inner fender wells.... <BR>hope someone can help...<BR>Thanx JAson<P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/dreamer.gif">    <BR><B>We all have dreams, but not everyone gets them...</B>||||reg||1
Z||000002||FieroLisa||12-05-2001||08:29 PM||||When I put 87 fascias on my 86 coupe, I needed to replace the inner fenders as well||||reg||1
Z||000003||Hartz||12-05-2001||09:17 PM||||Fiero Conversions is still in business?!?  Amazing...<P>As for your nose, yes you will need to either get the GT inner fender liners, or fab something to replicate what the factory did - which is really not too much more fancy than staple a piece of plastic/rubber to fill the gap.  The rest of the mounting holes should be the same, so you really just want to clean up the fender wells for apperance, and to keep water and dust out.<P>Best of luck,<P>Hartz<P>'84 SC 5.7L V8<BR>'85 SE 3.1L V6 Turbo<BR>hartzfeld.home.mindspring.com||||reg||1
Z||000004||Cheever3000||12-05-2001||09:46 PM||||That's what Michael Jackson said.  Oh, you meant... <BR>... nevermind.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000005||hutch84fierose||12-05-2001||10:16 PM||||well so i need to get fenders or the inner fender.  Well there must be something else that holds it on.  Theres the inner fenders, side marklikes, 7 screws on the hood what else is there i missing something.  There must be something hooks behind the inner fender.  has anyone ever done this conversion???<BR>Please help thanks alot||||reg||1
Z||000006||Hartz||12-05-2001||10:21 PM||||Yeah, I just did the conversion, but I am not sure I understand your question.  (If you're on the forum now, stop by chat!)<P>There are a bunch of fasteners that hold on the front fasica.  But there are also some fasteners which connect the inner fender liner to the front fasica.  I thought your question related to those?  The coupe inner fender liners and the aero nose inner fender liners are different.  The aero nose inner fender liners have a piece that (looks like it) is stapled to the fender liner, which extends the profile to meet the nose.  You either need to get a new inner fender liner, or fabricate some piece of plastic up to fill the gap.<P>Hartz<P>(Edited to fix the REALLY stupid grammer mistakes...)<p>[This message has been edited by Hartz (edited 12-05-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000007||88-DOHC||12-05-2001||10:58 PM||||There is actually a fair differences between a coupe nose, and a GT nose when converting from one to the other. Namely you have to change out the front fender wells, the honeycomb backing structure, the front air dam, replace/remove the paper-like air guides into the radiator, and may have to change out the hanging brackets where the bottom rear of the nose connects to (in front of each fender well). Hope that helps.<P>||||reg||1
Z||000008||Tigger||12-05-2001||11:24 PM||||There are also brackets that mount the bumper to the bottom of the radiator support and help support the black airdam.||||reg||1
Z||000009||hutch84fierose||12-06-2001||09:24 AM||||i think thats what im missing the brackets under the rad,  My old nose had a spoiler looking thing that attached to the bottom of that but what does this nose use.<BR>I don't know what i got my self into maybe i will take it back and forget this whole idea||||reg||1
Z||000010||Ali_Gulf_Fiero||12-06-2001||11:35 AM||||The underside skidplate bolts to the radiator support. The aero skidplate/air dam is one piece. Two curved brackets bolt to the plastic air dam part, which then screw into the bottom lip of the aero nose.<BR>First order of business would be to fabricate some sort of support for the bottom of your fascia. The side pieces stapled to the wheel well liners are merely cosmetic. You need to support the static weight of the nose and counter the increased downward/aft force generated at higher speeds. If you don't do so, you'll tear the urethane where the top nose-to-frame bracket meet. <BR>Make sure you have the correct energy absorber too.<BR>I'm not sure the funny little black plastic parts are crucial. Anyone?<P>------------------<BR>my cat's sitting in my scrambled eggs.||||reg||1
Z||000011||Ali_Gulf_Fiero||12-06-2001||11:50 AM||||Don't take it back! It IS a direct bolt on. You just need MORE bolts than you started with! (ha-ha)<BR>Time to raid the junkyards or get creative at the hardware store. It can be done. I have faith in you.<BR>The result is worth the inconvenience.||||reg||1
Z||000012||Voytek||12-06-2001||11:50 AM||||Ask Fiero Conversions to give you all the other parts to bolt on the nose.  I wouldn't count on getting the inner fender liners but you DEFINITELY need the bracket that goes underneath and attaches the nose to the radiator.  It has a plastic air dam on it too.<P>I would just tell them they forgot to ship you a part of the set.<P>The honeycomb inside the nose is also necessary.<P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/voyteks_sig2.gif"> ||||reg||1
